        en: '1226 — at 64 — I decided to attack Western Xia again.\n\nWhy a second campaign? Before my 1219 western campaign I had asked Western Xia for troops. King Li Zunxu (Emperor Shenzong, reigned 1211-1223) refused — and said "Chinggis Khan cannot subdue the west and attack us at the same time." His son Li Dewang took the throne in 1223 and I began planning the second Western Xia campaign. I held that grudge for 7 years.\n\nAutumn 1226 I led the army south — and besieged the Western Xia capital Yinchuan (modern Yinchuan, Ningxia).\n\nSummer 1227 — six-month siege — King Li Xian came out to surrender. But I was already gravely ill.\n\n25 August 1227 — I died at a military camp in the Liupan Mountains (southern Ningxia) — at 65.\n\nThe cause is murky in the sources. The Secret History (1240) says "fall from a horse" during a hunt. Marco Polo\'s Travels (1298) says "arrow wound" during the Western Xia campaign. Rashid al-Din\'s Compendium of Chronicles (1306) says "fever and plague." The Yuanshi (1370) says "summer heat-poison." Four sources, four versions. Careful framing: multiple causes of death for 13th-century rulers is normal — all four may be partly true (fall → infected wound → fever) — or all four may be later historiographic politics.\n\nMy will: massacre the entire Western Xia capital, kill the surrendered Li Xian, then bury me in secret — no tomb, no marker.\n\nThe burial site has never been found. Steppe tradition: cavalry trampled the funeral path; horses flattened the earth over the grave; grass grew. 21st-century archaeological teams (Mongolian, American, Japanese) have searched for over 30 years. Mongolian law explicitly forbids excavating possible Chinggis Khan tombs — it is a national taboo.',
