        en: 'Last — five cross-cultural perpetrator-actors — the same role across civilizations.\n\n1. Me — Chinggis Khan (1206 Kurultai) — steppe system-builder and architect of the western campaign that killed several million. Pax Mongolica trade routes for 100 years (1240-1340) versus mass killing. Double-edged. The same infrastructure became the Black Death\'s 1346 highway — Eurasia lost roughly a third of its population in the 1340s (see the Black Death topic).\n\n2. Mansa Musa (1324 Mali hajj) — 14th-century African wealth projector who marched 12,000 enslaved people 8,000 km (al-Umari 1342 gives no exact figure; later estimates fall around 10,000-12,000). Soft-power diplomacy versus trans-Saharan slave trafficking.\n\n3. Lorenzo de\' Medici (15th-century Florence) — Renaissance patron and political persecutor of rivals. Botticelli commissions versus post-1478 Pazzi-conspiracy reprisals (21st-century scholars estimate roughly 80 executions in the aftermath).\n\n4. Emperor Huizong (1100-1126) — artist-emperor and the man under whom the Northern Song collapsed (see the Tang-Song topic). Cultural peak versus political ruin.\n\n5. Justinian (6th-century Byzantium) — restorer of the Roman empire and orderer of the 532 Nika riot massacre (Procopius 6c gives the 30,000 figure; 21st-century estimates fall in the 10,000-30,000 range — see the Byzantine topic).\n\nFive people, five civilizations, 1,500 years. The pattern is the same: extreme system-building, systematic violence, same person.\n\nThis is not "they were all bad people." It is that the perpetrator-actor role exists in every civilization — and often achievement and violence inhabit the same body.\n\n21st-century AP DBQ training asks you to hold contradictions — see light and shadow at once — without flattening into binaries.\n\nIn spring 1206 at the source of the Onon I founded the Mongol Empire. In 1219-1221 my western campaign sacked four cities. Pax Mongolica is real. The western massacres are real.\n\nBoth are the same person.',
