        en: 'Last — five cross-civilizational lonely-mediators — the same role across cultures.\n\n1. Me — Yelü Chucai (13c Khitan) — wedged between Chinggis\'s steppe empire and Han agricultural civilization. Belonged to neither. In 1230 three ledger columns saved tens of millions of Han peasants — but I said it was not "for the Han" but "for Mongol fiscal sustainability."\n\n2. Ibn Battuta (14c Moroccan) — from 1325 spent 30 years traversing the Islamic world: North Africa, Middle East, Central Asia, India, Quanzhou in coastal China, Mali in West Africa. Dictated the Rihla on his return. Arab by birth, witness to nine civilizations — and back in Tangier he had become a stranger.\n\n3. Bartolomé de las Casas (16c Spanish friar) — sailed with the conquistadors in 1502, then at 49 in 1514 turned, and spent the next 50 years defending the indigenous Americans. Spanish by birth — but the Spanish court treated him as a traitor; the indigenous treated him as Spanish. Belonged to neither.\n\n4. Erasmus (16c Dutch) — humanist who refused to take sides between Luther and Rome. "I cannot choose — both sides err." The Lutherans called him a coward; Catholics called him "the egg Luther hatched." Died alone in 1536 — but every European centrist since the 16th century descends from him.\n\n5. Su Shi (11c Northern Song — see the Tang-Song topic) — wedged between Wang Anshi\'s New Party and Sima Guang\'s Old Party. The New Party found him too conservative; the Old Party found him insufficiently radical. Demoted by each in turn his whole life. He wrote the high peak of Song-dynasty ci poetry (Song lyric song-form). Loneliness was his soil.\n\nFive people, five civilizations, five centuries — same structure: bilingual broker, belonging to neither side, often dying lonely or in political defeat, with posthumous reputation far higher than the living one.\n\nThis is not "they were all good people." The lonely-mediator role exists in every civilization — and achievement and loneliness often inhabit the same body.\n\n21st-century AP DBQ training asks you to hold contradictions — see "the broker\'s value" and "the broker\'s cost" at once — without flattening into binaries.\n\nI met Chinggis in 1218. I saved tens of millions of Han peasants in 1230. I died lonely in 1244 under Töregene\'s sidelining. Pax Mongolica is real. Karakorum\'s four religions are real. So is my own "belonging to neither side."\n\nAll three are the same person.\n\nBut what my 1230 ledger saved was the Han peasantry of the north. The tens of millions of Southern Song Han fell with the dynasty in 1279 at the naval battle of Yashan — Wen Tianxiang martyred, Lu Xiufu carrying the boy emperor into the sea. That part I did not save. In 1271 Kublai named the new dynasty "Yuan" (from the Yijing line "great is the primal heaven"); the Yuan four-class system pinned the Han and the Southerners at the bottom. I, Yelü, died in 1244 — none of this I lived to see.\n\nIn your life — yourself — are you a link on this chain?',
